Hepatitis C (HCV) (Blood/Body Fluids ‘Parenteral’)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Chronic fatigue, History of IVDU/unscreened transfusions, Stigmata of cirrhosis, Positive HCV RNA

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-016
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of chronic right upper quadrant pain, new-onset peripheral oedema, and profound lethargy requiring urgent assessment for decompensated cirrhosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with a known history of remote intravenous drug use presenting with progressive abdominal distension, severe fatigue, and new bilateral leg swelling. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for acute hepatic decompensation and rule out impending variceal bleeding or spontaneous bacterial peritonitis prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been in a total brain fog for months, but this week my belly feels full and tight, and my ankles are suddenly huge."
History of Present Illness: Illness has been characterized by a prolonged, insidious phase of chronic generalized fatigue and mild cognitive impairment. Over the past 7 days, the patient describes an insidious onset of right upper quadrant fullness and discomfort that has escalated in intensity today. The patient additionally notes the development of new peripheral oedema over the last 72 hours, indicating potential progression to decompensated cirrhosis. The patient admits to a history of intravenous drug use in their 20s. Denies any current haematemesis.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with difficulty due to leg heaviness and profound fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Decreasing mobility and cognitive "fog" are making independent medication management difficult.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg (up 4kg from baseline)
General: Chronically ill-appearing, mild muscle wasting visible in the temporal regions, alert but slow to respond to questions.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals mild ascites with shifting dullness and right upper quadrant fullness without acute peritonism. Skin exam reveals early stigmata of chronic liver disease including spider angiomas on the upper chest and palmar erythema. Bilateral 2+ pitting oedema extending to the mid-calf. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Chronic fatigue, History of IVDU/unscreened transfusions, Stigmata of cirrhosis, and Positive HCV RNA (pending pathology confirmation).
Environmental Observations: Trip hazards present due to clutter; patient has reduced peripheral awareness.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular hepatotoxic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hepatitis C (HCV) (Chronic with early decompensation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Right-Sided Heart Failure (Cor Pulmonale) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal jugular venous pressure, clear lung bases, and the presence of specific hepatic stigmata (spider angiomas).

Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, abdominal rigidity, and severe acute pain, though ascitic fluid analysis would be definitive.

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Vague, dull fullness rather than sharp, severe localized pain, and absence of Murphy's sign or fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with progressive signs of portal hypertension and fluid retention (ascites, peripheral oedema) on a background of likely chronic, undiagnosed Hepatitis C. The acute escalation of abdominal fullness and new-onset oedema over a short timeframe required urgent clinical evaluation to assess the severity of volume overload, rule out an acute intra-abdominal infection, and assess neurological status for early encephalopathy. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a significant risk of unchecked fluid accumulation and missed early signs of catastrophic hepatic failure, satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a low-dose diuretic (e.g., Spironolactone 50mg daily) to initiate gentle fluid mobilization. Urgent referral for comprehensive liver pathology (LFTs, coags, HCV serology/RNA, U&Es) and an abdominal ultrasound to quantify ascites and screen for hepatocellular carcinoma.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly implementing a low-sodium diet and removing the salt shaker from the table, the patient will prevent further water retention; witnessing the visible reduction in ankle swelling over the next few days will provide powerful positive reinforcement to maintain this difficult dietary change.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None at this stage.
DME/Safety (Optional): Compression stockings (mild).
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to review bloods, ultrasound, and titrate diuretics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient vomits any blood, passes black/tarry stools, becomes confused, excessively sleepy, or develops severe, sudden abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of chronic HCV and cirrhosis is presumptive pending definitive virology and imaging; liver function is dynamic.
Clinical Course: The fluid retention indicates the liver is struggling to produce essential proteins. This requires careful, ongoing medical management, but modern HCV treatments are highly curative if initiated before end-stage failure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Because your liver is under strain, the blood vessels in your food pipe may be swollen. If you vomit any blood or pass dark, sticky stools, you must call an ambulance immediately as this is a life-threatening emergency.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has quietly scarred your liver over many years, creating resistance to blood flow and causing fluid to leak into your belly and legs. The water pill will help your kidneys flush out this excess fluid.
Actionable Steps: Take the water pill in the morning to avoid waking up at night. Weigh yourself every morning after using the toilet; if you gain more than 1kg in a day, contact your GP immediately.

